<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Żagań</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sagan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Żagań is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobr">Bóbr</a> river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>. It became the seat of a Piast duchy in the late 13th century, was sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> Wettins in 1472 and was later held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a>, who began its great baroque palace. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the prisoner-of-war camp Stalag Luft III, scene of the Great Escape, stood nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zagan.jpg" alt="Żagań" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krzysztof Golik · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palace_in_Zagan_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
